231},"overview",[233,234,235],"p",{},"Kinotic OS deploys to Kubernetes and provides several deployment configurations for different environments.",[229,237,239],{"id":238},"deployment-options","Deployment Options",[241,242,244],"h3",{"id":243},"helm-charts","Helm Charts",[233,246,247,248,252],{},"Production-ready Helm charts are available in ",[249,250,251],"code",{},"deployment\u002Fhelm\u002F"," for deploying:",[254,255,256,264,270],"ul",{},[257,258,259,263],"li",{},[260,261,262],"strong",{},"Kinotic Server"," — The core platform with configurable replicas, resource limits, and ingress",[257,265,266,269],{},[260,267,268],{},"Elasticsearch"," — Search and persistence cluster",[257,271,272,275],{},[260,273,274],{},"Load Generator"," — For performance testing",[241,277,279],{"id":278},"docker-compose-local-development","Docker Compose (Local Development)",[233,281,282,283,286],{},"A Docker Compose configuration in ",[249,284,285],{},"deployment\u002Fdocker-compose\u002F"," provides a complete local development environment including the Kinotic server, Elasticsearch, and supporting services. This is the recommended way to run Kinotic OS during development.",[241,288,290],{"id":289},"kind-kubernetes-in-docker","KinD (Kubernetes in Docker)",[233,292,293,294,297],{},"For testing Kubernetes deployments locally, ",[249,295,296],{},"deployment\u002Fkind\u002F"," provides a KinD setup with Terraform configurations that deploy the full stack into a local Kubernetes cluster.",[241,299,301],{"id":300},"cloud-providers-terraform","Cloud Providers (Terraform)",[233,303,304,305,308],{},"Terraform configurations in ",[249,306,307],{},"deployment\u002Fterraform\u002F"," support deployment to:",[254,310,311,317],{},[257,312,313,316],{},[260,314,315],{},"AWS"," — EC2-based deployment with configurable instance types",[257,318,319,322],{},[260,320,321],{},"Azure"," — VM-based deployment on Azure infrastructure",[324,325,327],"h4",{"id":326},"ui-sites-on-azure","UI sites on Azure",[233,329,330,333,334,337,338,341,342,345,346,349,350,352,353,356,357,360,361,365,366,369,370,373,374,377],{},[249,331,332],{},"frontdoor.tf"," calls ",[249,335,336],{},"deployment\u002Fterraform\u002Fazure\u002Fmodules\u002Fsites",", which creates everything a\npublished UI needs, once per environment, so nothing on Front Door, in DNS or in storage is\ncreated when a UI is published: the sites storage account ",[249,339,340],{},"st\u003Cprefix>sites"," every site's\nfiles live in under ",[249,343,344],{},"sites\u002F\u003Chostname>\u002F",", the Front Door Standard profile and endpoint every\nsite is served through, a Let's Encrypt wildcard certificate for ",[249,347,348],{},"*.apps.\u003Czone>"," issued into\nthe cluster key vault by a DNS challenge and renewed by an apply within 30 days of expiry,\nthe wildcard custom domain and DNS record, an origin group that reads the account as the\nprofile's managed identity, and the route whose rules serve ",[249,351,344],{}," for a\nrequest's host. The module grants that identity Storage Blob Data Reader on the account and\nthe kinotic-server identity Storage Blob Data Contributor, with which the server signs the\nURLs the publish and removal workloads act through, and ",[249,354,355],{},"kinotic.tf"," passes the sites domain\nand the account's blob endpoint as ",[249,358,359],{},"KINOTIC_SYSTEMAPI_UIDEPLOYMENT_*"," environment variables\n(see ",[362,363,157],"a",{"href":364},"\u002Fplatform\u002Fconfiguration#ui-sites","). The certificate is issued by the\nprincipal terraform runs as, which needs ",[249,367,368],{},"lets_encrypt_email"," set and holds Key Vault\nCertificates Officer on the vault; the profile's identity is unknown until the profile\nexists, so a first apply targets ",[249,371,372],{},"module.sites.azurerm_cdn_frontdoor_profile.sites"," before\napplying the rest. The ",[249,375,376],{},"kinotic-server"," values file for development disables the provisioner\ninstead.",[233,379,380,381,384,385,388,389,393],{},"A developer machine gets the same module from ",[249,382,383],{},"deployment\u002Fterraform\u002Fazure\u002Fdev",", under\n",[249,386,387],{},"apps-\u003Cenvironment>.\u003Czone>"," with a key vault of its own. The\n",[362,390,392],{"href":391},"\u002Fplatform\u002Fcontributing#publishing-uis-against-azure","contributing guide"," walks through it.",{"title":395,"searchDepth":396,"depth":396,"links":397},"",2,[398,399],{"id":231,"depth":396,"text":25},{"id":238,"depth":396,"text":239,"children":400},[401,403,404,405],{"id":243,"depth":402,"text":244},3,{"id":278,"depth":402,"text":279},{"id":289,"depth":402,"text":290},{"id":300,"depth":402,"text":301},"Deploying and operating Kinotic OS in various environments.","md",null,{},{"icon":6},{"title":153,"description":406},"nCUD-e7IuV2tgBD1clMh-CSXDCl1ZNqxTJXtys13n_Y",[414,416],{"title":148,"path":149,"stem":150,"description":415,"icon":151,"children":-1},"High-level architecture of the Kinotic OS platform.",{"title":157,"path":158,"stem":159,"description":417,"icon":160,"children":-1},"Configuration reference for Kinotic OS server and services.",1788943109137]